today we will be redrawing the new cgi  dragon ball movie in traditional 2d  styles the new super dragon ball super  movie dragon ball super super hero will  be hitting our screens this summer and  apart from looking like a really  interesting development for underused  characters like piccolo goten trunks and  gohan it's also the very first dragon  ball film to be produced entirely using  computer generated visuals save for  perhaps some small moments in the recent  films traditionally dragon ball has  existed only in two dimensions with the  games being the only venture into the 3d  arena and while my heart will always  belong to traditional hand-drawn 2d  animation this new film looks great with  some really interesting approaches taken  to help bridge the divide between 3d and  2d audiences with all of that said  however it did get us thinking all of  these new trailers action sequences and  characters coloring our screens were  wonderful but we did wonder what might  this movie look like if it were created  using traditional 2d visuals instead of  cgi it's an interesting question for  sure but there is a problem what style  would we draw it in over the last 30  years dragon ball's visual identity has  changed more times than i can count with  a host of different artists giving their  own unique and iconic spins on the  franchise and that's when we had an idea  what if instead of focusing on just one  style we did all of them what if we  select a bunch of images from the  trailers gave each of them to a  different artist on the tnm team to  tackle with each drawing needing to be  drawn in a different iconic dragon ball  style from classic toriyama all the way  to shintani's dragon ball super broly  designs sounds like a fun video right  well no  god this was a nightmare and to make  matters worse i think i've developed  ptsd from a particular drawing i'll let  you guys figure out which one that was  so let's dive in  the first image up in the chopping block  to draw is this close-up from the first  trailer with sun gohan a simple enough  one to start things off with and the  first artist to draw is  [Music]  me oh joy and i have to redraw this  picture of gohan in the style of  dragon ball super  let's try it out alright so this one was  one of the easier drawings i did for the  video and to be honest i was really  happy with how it turned out there were  virtually no changes i needed to make to  the anatomy of the original cgi image  and so really the only alterations that  needed to be made were on a stylistic  front with this being the most modern of  the current styles of the series i was  excited to see how this one would turn  out in the end and maybe in some  alternate timeline the movie would have  followed in the visual style of the  broly film but who knows for now this is  as close as we're gonna get one of the  most important parts of the broly film  style has to be its line art in order to  achieve this varied weight look the film  did i had to do my line art with a fancy  one pixel thick brush with zero aliasing  if you don't know what that is just know  that it looks like ass until a final  filter is applied speaking of which  special thanks to friend of the channel  mix who helped apply this filter without  him this simply just wouldn't have  worked so let's take a look at the  finished product one two three  awesome looks legit thanks once again to  mix for helping with some of the images  in this video and so the next image we  have to draw is  oh this is an interesting one the new  character gamma number one and which one  of us will have to tackle this  editor sand and the style  classic toriyama to tell you the truth  this was the image i was most excited to  see produced not just because i'm a fan  of the design or toriyama specifically  but because this was the only image that  was drawn and produced using physical  non-digital traditional manga tools in  this video editor stan is an enthusiast  for manga production and quote does not  recommend buying the screen tones or any  such products unless they are either  available in your country or you are  very keen on becoming a professional and  spending a lot of money though if you  are interested there is apparently this  really interesting video with the  legendary naoki urasawa demonstrating  how to add screen tones to produce this  editor san needed to margin the page  accordingly sketch the layout ink the  final piece using the traditional tools  and finally physically cut out his own  screen tones to add to the finished  product i think my favorite aspect of  this whole process that editor-san is  undergoing is pretty much what i like  about this video it's taking a peek back  into the past using different styles on  modern material physical manga creation  is becoming more and more outdated as  time matches on and heck toriyama  himself even started to draw digitally  over a decade ago and it makes sense the  materials can be expensive and digital  is far more convenient still though just  looking at this there's something so  interesting and different about this  approach and one i was very interested  in being honored in this video  fantastic work editors and that's one  fantastic looking two-page spread next  up our third image and on this list is  the lovely cgi close-up of vegeta  and our next artist to tackle it is  trev who will have to redraw it in the  style of  modern tarayoshi yamamuro otherwise  known as dragon ball super's art style  tadayoshi yamamuro caught his teeth with  early dragon ball before going on to  become one of the most iconic animators  and character designers for the final  arc of z as well as all of dragon ball  super however he is not present in this  film as far as we are aware the  character designer and animation  director for dragon ball super superhero  is an individual called chikashi kubota  most will know him as one punch man's  character designer but he's also a huge  dragon ball fan having worked in dragon  ball super broly and even releasing his  own dragon ball art book it's obvious he  knows his dragon ball interestingly his  role in this film will be to touch up  the 3d models with 2d draw overs those  with a keen eye while watching battle of  gods and resurrection f saw that in  those cgi sequences things didn't look  quite right as dragon ball doesn't quite  work in 3d without changing up the model  dramatically and so in superhero thanks  to 2d corrections to the faces we get  stunning shots of goku and other  characters in the trailers that while  primarily still cg can remain true to  the design philosophy of dragon ball but  overall this drawing itself was no  challenge for trev and so it's  interesting getting to see what this  might have looked like had it been in  dragon ball super back in the day good  work trev and next up is this lovely  image of piccolo so who's going to be  drawing this lovely fella  me okay and the style is  toyotaro  okay  uh this is gonna be tough and tough it  most definitely was i've had my  criticisms of toyotaro in the past but  he actually draws namekians and piccolo  very very well and i'm nowhere near as  talented as he is regrettably and if  that wasn't all this cgi image i'm  attempting to redraw presented its own  challenges i've mentioned a few times in  this video that when you translate a 2d  design like dragon ball into 3d space it  doesn't always work too smoothly and for  whatever reason the angle piccolo's head  was in this image i just could not get  right eventually however thanks to some  help from trev i was able to land on  something that we were all happy with i  tried to emulate toyotaro's detail in  the arms as well as implement the  typical shapes he uses for the facial  features and clothing folds in the end i  was really happy with how it turned out  and when you compared with the original  i think the difference is quite stark  and while this one was tough it wasn't  anywhere near as hard as things got for  me in this video but more on that later  next image up for the redraw is oh this  is a nice upshot of gohan in his super  saiyan form and redrawing this image  will be trev again and in the style of  classic tadayoshi yamamuro or as many of  you might be familiar with the style  that defined much of the buu saga this  style is trev's bread and butter and  while he could emulate this style in his  sleep much the same way i had difficulty  with the previous image trev 2 had  difficulty translating this 3d angle  into dragon ball's 2d philosophy and  there's an interesting reason as to why  the issue with dragon ball in 3d is that  toriyama's designs don't actually make  sense in three-dimensional space so what  do i mean when i say that well consider  goku's hair for example in 2d it  functions much like mickey mouse's ears  in that it just flips rather than  rotating in 3d this couldn't work  similarly and this is where this angle  has given us trouble the manner with  which various characters faces compress  at three-quarter angles doesn't really  make sense in 2d when akira toriyama  designed these characters he did so  without consideration for what they  would look like in three-dimensional  space naturally this isn't to say that  it's impossible to get around these  issues though and there are a few ways  of doing it when you look at some of the  older video game 3d models they were  true to 3d models and as a result the  faces at angles didn't really look great  or at least not in the way toriyama  would have drawn them and so to remedy  this problem artists would shift and  warp the faces at certain angles to  create a better likeness to the original  designs effectively breaking their 3d  models momentarily in the process while  dragon ball fighterz was one of the most  recent examples of one that does this in  real time one of the most amazing  instances that's perfected pretty much  every element is dragon ball legends  intro video it's all a bit fast and hard  to read since it's a short op but if you  look at all the elements it's kind of  incredible but with that said i think  trev managed to find a nice happy medium  with this angle compressing the face to  create a faithful adaptation of this  image true to the design philosophy of  the time and of course true to the  classic teddy yoshi yamamuro from the  buu saga amazing work trev next up this  monstrosity and i had to draw it in the  style of masaki sato uh for those of you  that might not be familiar with masaki  sato he's the man behind some of my  favorite illustrations from the original  run of dragon ball and dragon ball z on  tv he's behind some of the most iconic  scenes in the series not limited to goku  vs piccolo krillin throwing the spirit  bomb of vegeta and of course a little  scene you might have heard of goku's  first transformation into super saiyan  to name but a few he's featured in  various films and overall is known for  his rugged and detailed approach and it  made me want to unalive myself everyone  else that worked on this video spent  maybe a few hours at most in a single  drawing this one took me three days and  is the reason why this video is as late  as it was and was it worth it nothing  was worth this everything about this was  working against me the angle his face  was at was bizarre not quite looking  right at you not quite looking up not  quite three quarters an angle made for  3d and designed to destroy my spirit it  was the pose was also unconventional and  i had to use every morsel of willpower i  had to not break my ipad that i was  drawing on into but really the most  difficult aspect of this was the design  itself and adapting it the film uses  designs that are quite modern toriyama  in approach from the rounder shapes all  the way to the soft shading which made  even the shading of this drawing a  difficult enterprise to figure out as  masaki sado's style is so different to  the philosophy on display here with its  being angular detailed and layered and  even its shading even with the help of  all the other artists in this video this  one broke my spirit i managed to  eventually get to something where we  were all happy but in the end this image  unequivocally defeated me  but that seems sort of depressing to end  on a high note here's trevory drawing  goku in a style of yuya takahashi making  it look insultingly easy it even  demonstrates how goku's hair when not  touched up manually doesn't really look  quite right in 3d if this video has  taught me anything it's that i am  nowhere near as talented as the artists  that have worked on this film and indeed  the prior incarnations of the series  these are all people that deserve our  respect and i'm excited to see what this  new dragon ball super movie has in store  for us this summer taking on toriyama's  style in 3d is no easy feat so seeing  how staff are attempting to overcome the  nuances required to make it look right  is a fascinating exercise and one i'm  excited to see once the movie's out with  that said i'm always looking for reasons  to keep drawing and making these videos  and so since a new trailer just dropped  with tons of interesting new shots and  angles i figured a part 2 to this video  is in order and so next week you will  get more redraws from this film from me  and the team but we're going to need  some help with this and this is where  you come in we need suggestions so in  order for you to be in with a chance for  your suggestions to make it into next  week's video all you gotta do is  subscribe to this channel follow me on  twitter totally not mark and share your  suggestions using the hashtag dbs redraw  just make sure it's a still from the  trailer of dragon ball super superhero i  look forward to seeing your suggestions  and i'll see you all next week thank you  so much for watching  [Music]  you

